---
// The explanation below the Lab: the data, the symbols, how a line is
// measured, how the best line is found (the optimization), and the model in
// short. Static HTML; formulas are rendered by KaTeX at build time.
import { formatNumber } from '../../lib/format';
import { renderTex } from '../../lib/katex';
import { datasets } from './data';
import { equations } from './equations';
import { predict } from './math';

const tex = Object.fromEntries(
  equations.map((e) => [e.id, { html: renderTex(e.tex), words: e.words }]),
);
const dataset = datasets[0];
const first = dataset?.reference[0];
const example = first ? predict(first, dataset.exampleX) : NaN;
---

<div class="explain">
  <section aria-labelledby="explain-data">
    <h2 id="explain-data">What you are looking at</h2>
    <p>
      Each dot is one measurement of a growing plant. Across the graph (x) is
      the plant's age in weeks; up the graph (y) is its height in centimetres.
      The table lists the same 10 measurements, one per row.
    </p>
    <p>
      The numbers are made up. Each plant starts between 1 and 10 cm tall and
      grows between 1 and 10 cm a week, and every measurement is a little off,
      just as real measurements are. <strong>Refresh Data</strong> shows a
      different plant, with its own start and its own growth.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="explain-symbols">
    <h2 id="explain-symbols">What each symbol means</h2>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <div>
        <dt>x</dt>
        <dd>The input we know: the plant's age, in weeks.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>y</dt>
        <dd>The real result: the plant's measured height.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-line">ŷ</dt>
        <dd>
          "y-hat": the line's prediction of the height. The hat means
          "estimated".
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-a">a</dt>
        <dd>
          The intercept: the height of the line at week 0, when the plant was
          first measured. It moves the whole line up or down.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-b">b</dt>
        <dd>
          The slope: how many centimetres the plant grows each week. It tilts
          the line.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-error">e</dt>
        <dd>
          The miss for one point: e = y − ŷ. Positive when the point is above
          the line, negative when it is below.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>MSE</dt>
        <dd>
          Mean squared error: the average of all the misses, each one squared.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Typical miss</dt>
        <dd>
          The square root of the MSE. It is back in centimetres, so it reads as
          "the line is usually off by about this much".
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="explain-error">
    <h2 id="explain-error">How good is a line?</h2>
    <p>
      No straight line goes through every point, so every line misses a little.
      The miss for one point is the gap between the real value and the
      prediction:
    </p>
    <div class="formula" set:html={tex.miss?.html} />
    <p>
      To score the whole line, square every miss and take the average. Squaring
      makes every miss count as positive, so misses above and below the line
      cannot cancel out, and it punishes big misses more than small ones:
    </p>
    <div class="formula" set:html={tex.mse?.html} />
    <p>
      The smaller the MSE, the better the line fits. Turn on{' '}
      <strong>Misses</strong> to see each e as a dotted line from the point to
      your line.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="explain-best">
    <h2 id="explain-best">Finding the best line: the optimization</h2>
    <p>
      Training a linear regression model means choosing a and b so that the MSE
      is as small as possible. That search is the optimization.
    </p>
    <p>
      Imagine trying every pair of a and b and writing down the MSE of each
      line. The results form a smooth bowl with exactly one lowest point, and
      that point is the best line. For a straight line, two formulas jump
      straight to it:
    </p>
    <div class="formula" set:html={tex.best?.html} />
    <p>
      x̄ and ȳ are the averages of x and y, so the best line always passes
      through the average point. Turn on <strong>Best Line</strong> to compare
      it with yours. Because every measurement is a little off, the best line
      lands close to the plant's real start and growth, but rarely exactly on
      them: the line can only learn from the data it sees.
    </p>
    <p>
      Bigger models have no such formula. There, the computer walks down the
      bowl instead: it nudges a and b a little in the direction that lowers the
      error, again and again, until the error stops shrinking. This is called
      gradient descent, and it is how most machine-learning models are trained.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="explain-summary">
    <h2 id="explain-summary">The model in short</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Choose a line:</strong> ŷ = a + b·x turns any age into a
        predicted height.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Measure how wrong it is:</strong> square every miss and average
        them (the MSE).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Optimize:</strong> find the a and b that make the MSE as small
        as possible.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Predict:</strong> for a new x, read ŷ off the line. For the
        first plant, the best line predicts that at 4 weeks old it is about{' '}
        {formatNumber(example, 0)} cm tall.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</div>

<style>
  .explain {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-12);
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: var(--fs-16);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  section {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-4);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: var(--fs-24);
  }

  .formula {
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding-block: var(--s-2);
    font-size: var(--fs-18);
  }

  .symbols {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .symbols div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 7.5rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-4);
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  .symbols dt {
    font-family: 'KaTeX_Math', 'Times New Roman', serif;
    font-size: var(--fs-20);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  /* MSE and "Typical miss" are words, not symbols. */
  .symbols div:nth-last-child(-n + 2) dt {
    font-family: inherit;
    font-size: var(--fs-16);
    font-style: normal;
  }

  .symbols dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .steps {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
    padding-inline-start: 1.4em;
  }

  @media (width < 560px) {
    .symbols div {
      grid-template-columns: 5.5rem minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--s-3);
    }
  }
</style>
